page("chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill","Charcoal Chicken Thighs on a Compact Grill","Juicy bone-in, skin-on chicken thighs on the Slot Grill Compact: start on the cool side, crisp over the coals, sauce at the end. With timing, temps and fixes.",IMG["c13"],recipe2(
"<p>Bone-in, skin-on chicken thighs are the most forgiving chicken you can put over charcoal. They have enough fat to stay juicy, they're cheap, and they taste better cooked well past the minimum temperature. On a small grill the challenge is the skin: fat renders, drips onto the coals and flares. Start them on the cool side of a two-zone fire, let them render and cook through gently, then crisp the skin over the coals at the end.</p>",
"2–3 (6 thighs)","About 45–55 minutes",
["6 bone-in, skin-on chicken thighs","2 tablespoons of your favorite rub, or 1½ teaspoons salt plus pepper, garlic powder and paprika","1 tablespoon oil","BBQ sauce (optional)","Lemon wedges or hot sauce for serving"],
["<b>Dry and season.</b> Pat the thighs dry. Trim big flaps of loose skin and fat. Rub with oil and season all over. For extra-crisp skin, season and refrigerate uncovered for a few hours or overnight.","<b>Light a chimney</b> (fuller than a burger cook) and build a two-zone fire with a wide cool zone.","<b>Start skin-side up on the cool side.</b> Arrange the thighs over the thin or empty end with the thickest part toward the coals.","<b>Cook gently,</b> turning and rotating every 8–10 minutes so each piece takes a turn closest to the heat. Add a few coals to the hot side after 20–30 minutes.","<b>Check temperature.</b> When the thighs reach about 165°F in the thickest part (not touching bone), they're safe. Thighs are juicier and more tender at about 175–185°F.","<b>Crisp the skin.</b> Move them skin-side down over the hot coals for 1–3 minutes. Watch closely and move any that flare.","<b>Sauce at the end</b> (optional). Brush sauce on and give each side about a minute over medium heat. Sugar burns, so never sauce early.","<b>Rest</b> 5 minutes and serve."],
why="<ul><li>The cool zone renders fat gently without burning.</li><li>The thick steel firebox holds steady heat for a 45-minute cook.</li><li>Small grill, small batch: six thighs fill the grate with room to move them.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Lemon-garlic:</b> marinate in lemon juice, garlic, oregano and olive oil for 2–4 hours.","<b>Spicy:</b> chili powder, cayenne and smoked paprika rub; finish with hot honey.","<b>Boneless thighs:</b> cook mostly over medium heat, 4–6 minutes per side; they're quicker and flare less.","<b>Drumsticks:</b> same method; allow a bit longer and turn more often.","<b>Wood flavor:</b> add a small chunk of untreated hardwood to the coals."],
trouble=[("Skin is flabby","Not enough rendering time on the cool side, or skin was wet. Dry well; crisp longer at the end."),("Burnt outside, raw inside","Too long over direct heat. Start on the cool side; finish with heat."),("Sauce burned","Sauced too early or over high heat. Sauce in the last few minutes over medium."),("Flare-ups","Move pieces to the cool side; trim excess fat.")],
serve="<p>Serve with slaw, grilled corn or <a href='/grilled-vegetables-compact-grill.html'>grilled vegetables</a>. Leftover thighs are excellent shredded into tacos or salads. Refrigerate within 2 hours and reheat to 165°F.</p>"),"recipes",
[("How long do chicken thighs take on a charcoal grill?","Bone-in thighs usually take about 35 to 45 minutes using a two-zone fire, but always cook to temperature rather than time."),("What temperature should chicken thighs be cooked to?","165°F is the USDA safe minimum for poultry. Thighs are often juicier and more tender at about 175 to 185°F."),("How do I get crispy chicken skin on a small grill?","Dry the skin well, start the thighs skin-side up on the cool side to render, then finish skin-side down over the hot coals for a minute or two."),("When should I add BBQ sauce?","In the last few minutes of cooking. Sugar in sauce burns quickly over charcoal.")])

page("chicken-wings-small-charcoal-grill","Charcoal Chicken Wings on a Small Grill","Crispy, smoky wings on the Slot Grill Compact: dry, season, cook indirect, crisp direct, then toss in sauce. Buffalo, lemon pepper and dry-rub options.",IMG["c13"],recipe2(
"<p>Wings over charcoal beat wings from almost anywhere else: smoky, crisp, with rendered skin and a little char. They're also ideal for a small grill, because they're small pieces you can rotate around a two-zone fire, and they hold well, so you can cook in waves for a <a href='/cooking-for-a-crowd-small-grill.html'>crowd</a>. The method: cook gently on the cool side until rendered, crisp over the coals, toss in sauce off the grill.</p>",
"2–3 (about 2 lb)","About 45 minutes",
["About 2 lb chicken wings, split into flats and drums, tips removed","1½ teaspoons salt","1 teaspoon black pepper","1 teaspoon garlic powder","1 teaspoon baking powder (optional, aluminum-free, helps crisping)","For Buffalo sauce: ½ cup hot sauce and 4 tablespoons melted butter","For lemon pepper: zest of 1 lemon, 2 teaspoons cracked pepper, 2 tablespoons melted butter","Celery, carrots and ranch or blue cheese dressing"],
["<b>Dry the wings thoroughly</b> with paper towels. For the crispiest skin, refrigerate uncovered on a rack for a few hours or overnight.","<b>Season</b> with salt, pepper, garlic powder and baking powder (if using). Toss to coat evenly.","<b>Light a full chimney</b> and build a two-zone fire with a wide cool zone.","<b>Arrange wings on the cool side</b> in a single layer, thicker drums closest to the coals.","<b>Cook 25–30 minutes,</b> turning and rotating every 7–8 minutes. Add a few coals to the hot side partway through.","<b>Crisp.</b> Move wings over the hot coals a few at a time for 1–2 minutes per side, until blistered and golden. Watch for flare-ups.","<b>Check temperature.</b> Wings are safe at 165°F, and most cooks prefer them at about 175–190°F for tender, rendered meat.","<b>Toss in sauce</b> in a bowl off the grill, and serve immediately."],
why="<ul><li>Small pieces fit the 146 sq in grate well and are easy to rotate between zones.</li><li>Charcoal smoke and direct heat give flavor and crisp skin.</li><li>Wings hold well in a covered pan at the cool end while the next batch cooks.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Dry rub:</b> skip sauce; use your favorite BBQ rub before cooking and a sprinkle after.","<b>Honey-garlic:</b> warm honey, soy sauce, garlic and a splash of vinegar; toss after cooking.","<b>Hot honey:</b> honey warmed with chili flakes, tossed at the end.","<b>Lemon pepper:</b> toss in lemon butter, then sprinkle with zest and pepper."],
trouble=[("Rubbery skin","Not dried well or not enough time on the cool side. Dry overnight; render longer; crisp over coals."),("Burned before done","Too much direct heat; keep them on the cool side until nearly done."),("Sauce slides off","Toss while wings are hot, in a bowl, with sauce that contains butter or fat.")],
serve="<p>Serve with celery, carrots and dressing. Leftover wings re-crisp nicely over a gentle fire. Refrigerate within 2 hours.</p>"),"recipes",
[("How long do wings take on a charcoal grill?","About 30 to 40 minutes total on a two-zone fire: 25 to 30 minutes on the cool side, then a few minutes over the coals to crisp. Cook to temperature."),("What temperature should wings reach?","165°F is safe for poultry; many cooks take wings to about 175 to 190°F for more tender, rendered meat."),("Should I sauce wings on the grill?","Toss them in sauce in a bowl after cooking. Sugary sauces burn quickly over charcoal."),("How do I make wings crispy on a grill?","Dry them well, use a little baking powder in the seasoning if you like, render on the cool side, then crisp over direct heat.")])

page("pork-chops-small-charcoal-grill","Thick-Cut Pork Chops on a Small Charcoal Grill","Juicy bone-in pork chops on the Slot Grill Compact: brine or dry brine, sear hot, finish on the cool side, pull at 145°F. Glazes, sides and fixes.",IMG["c08"],recipe2(
"<p>Pork chops have a reputation for coming out dry, and it's almost always because they were cooked too long or too hot the whole way. Modern pork is lean and is safe at 145°F with a 3-minute rest, so a thick chop can be juicy and slightly blush in the center. A two-zone fire on the Slot Grill Compact makes it easy: hard sear for flavor, gentle finish so you don't overshoot.</p>",
"2","About 30 minutes, plus brining",
["2 bone-in pork chops, 1–1½\" thick","Simple brine (optional): 4 cups water and ¼ cup salt, plus 2 tablespoons sugar","Or dry brine: about ¾ teaspoon salt per chop","Black pepper, garlic powder, a little brown sugar or your rub","Oil","Optional glaze: 2 tablespoons honey or maple syrup, 1 tablespoon mustard, 1 teaspoon vinegar"],
["<b>Brine.</b> Wet brine for 1–2 hours in the fridge, or dry brine (salt both sides) for at least 40 minutes up to overnight. Rinse wet-brined chops and dry well.","<b>Light a full chimney</b> and build a two-zone fire.","<b>Pat dry, oil and season.</b> Skip extra salt if brined.","<b>Sear over the hot zone</b> about 2–3 minutes per side, until well browned. Sear the fat edge briefly with tongs.","<b>Slide to the cool side.</b> Flip every couple of minutes and check the center with an instant-read thermometer.","<b>Glaze (optional)</b> in the last 2–3 minutes, brushing both sides.","<b>Pull at 145°F,</b> rest 3 minutes, and serve."],
why="<ul><li>Close, hot coals give a fast crust before the inside overcooks.</li><li>A cool zone works as the gentle finish.</li><li>A griddle on the other half handles apples, onions or potatoes in the same fire.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Boneless chops:</b> cook faster; watch the thermometer closely.","<b>Apple and onion:</b> slice and cook on the <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a> with butter while the chops finish.","<b>Spice-rubbed:</b> paprika, cumin, garlic and brown sugar.","<b>Pork tenderloin:</b> same idea; turn every few minutes and pull at 145°F."],
trouble=[("Dry chops","Overcooked. Pull at 145°F; brine next time."),("Glaze burned","Added too early or over the hot side; glaze at the end on the cool side."),("Curling edges","Cut small slits through the fat cap before cooking.")],
serve="<p>Serve with grilled apples, onions or <a href='/grilled-vegetables-compact-grill.html'>vegetables</a>. Slice leftovers thin for sandwiches. Refrigerate within 2 hours.</p>"),"recipes",
[("What temperature should pork chops be cooked to?","USDA's safe minimum for pork chops is 145°F with a 3-minute rest."),("How long do thick pork chops take on a charcoal grill?","Usually about 2 to 3 minutes per side over hot coals, then several more minutes on the cool side, depending on thickness. Cook to temperature."),("Should I brine pork chops?","Brining (wet or dry) helps keep lean chops juicy and seasoned. Even 40 minutes of dry brining helps."),("How do I keep pork chops from drying out?","Use thick chops, brine them, sear and then finish on the cool side, and pull at 145°F.")])
